; E170 lane E -- THE ZEROS. Seven semantically WRONG implementations that the
; port types ACCEPT, and this file compiling is the measurement.
;
; ⚑ THIS FIXTURE IS NOT A CONTROL AND NOT A DEMO. Lane E's operational test --
; a port type carries meaning iff there exists a wrong implementation satisfying
; its shape that the type rejects -- has a negative answer for facets of every
; port here, and a lane that reported only its refusals would be reporting half
; a measurement. So each zero is committed as a definition that TYPECHECKS, and
; Phase 12 asserts that it does. The row is not decoration: the day one of these
; is refused -- E171 lands, a carrier registry closes the forgery, a profile
; hands out the caps -- this fixture goes RED and the per-port map in
; .planning/RUNG1-CHECKLIST.md has to be re-measured instead of quietly rotting.
; That is the whole reason a zero is written as an assertion rather than a note.
;
; The seven, and what each one measures:
;
;   Z1 pe-send-noop   THE PAYLOAD IS UNPINNED, and it is the widest zero in the
;                     lane. A `sock-send` stand-in that reports success and
;                     sends nothing has the crossing's exact type, conserves the
;                     capability, and is accepted. No port type in the tree says
;                     anything about what a crossing DOES -- only about who may
;                     hold the authority to do it and how the handle moves.
;   Z2 pe-forge-fd    NON-FORGEABILITY IS ZERO for Fd. `adopt-fd : (=> I64 Fd)`
;                     (ports/fd.chiral:32) mints the capability from an arbitrary
;                     integer, so holding an `Fd` is evidence of nothing. The
;                     same shape mints every other cap in the tree:
;                     `adopt-pty : (=> I64 Pty)`, `env-open : (=> Unit Env)`,
;                     `secret-seal`, `backend-open`, `pool-create`,
;                     `sock-connect`, `sock-listen`, `socketpair`.
;   Z3 pe-ambient-w   HOLDING THE CAP IS NOT REQUIRED to do the thing the cap
;                     names. `write-fd : (=> I64 Bytes I64)` performs the real
;                     write(2) on a bare integer, beside `fd-close`, which takes
;                     `(1 f Fd)`. So the Fd cap governs DISPOSAL and not USE, and
;                     42 of the 62 crossings in scaffold/lib take no capability
;                     parameter at all. This is the no-ambient-authority gap the
;                     port bank names (docs/banks/port.md, shard 3), as a
;                     program.
;   Z4 pe-pure-write  THE `->`/`=>` MEMBRANE REFUSES NOTHING AT THE CALL. A PURE
;                     arrow performs a crossing. ⚑ This is a SHARPER measurement
;                     than the one E171 was minted on: that one had a `->` def
;                     calling a `=>` DEF (docs/banks/port.md shard 3, third leg,
;                     2026-08-25); this one calls the `=>` EXTERN directly, so
;                     not even the declared crossing itself is confined to an
;                     effectful context. Enforcement is E171, unbuilt.
;   Z5 pe-pool-oob    THE VALUE INDEX PINS SIZE IDENTITY, NOT OFFSET VALIDITY.
;                     `(Pool 4096)` is a different type from `(Pool 16384)` --
;                     e170_reject_pool_size.chiral proves the type refuses the
;                     confusion -- but a write at offset 999999 into a 4096-byte
;                     pool compiles. The refinement machinery that WOULD decide
;                     it exists (`mem-put-checked`); `pool-write`'s own type does
;                     not use it.
;   Z6 pe-reap-dup    THE OMEGA GUARD IS PORTTYPE-ONLY, so a cap-shaped `data`
;                     can be DUPLICATED. `(w s Sock)` is refused outright
;                     ("function parameter of a linear type at quantity omega");
;                     `(w r Reap)` is accepted, and one `Reap` becomes two. Reap
;                     is a live obligation -- proc.chiral:25, "dropping it is a
;                     type error" -- and duplicating it means two waits on one
;                     child. Named in test-e168-floor.sh's G8 comment as a
;                     measured gap decision 10 takes on purpose; this is the
;                     exploit, committed.
;
;   Z7 pe-send-after   PROTOCOL STATE IS NOT IN THE TYPE, beyond the Sock/LSock
;                     split. `recv-closed (1 sock Sock)` (ports/sock.chiral:24)
;                     hands back a PLAIN `Sock`, so sending on a socket the peer
;                     has already closed typechecks. docs/banks/port.md shard 8
;                     says "do not assert ports are session-typed"; this is that
;                     sentence as a program, and it is the reason the map's
;                     `Sock` row does not get a protocol column.
;
; ⚑ AN EIGHTH ZERO THAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN AS A DEFINITION, so it is asserted by
; the enumeration guard in test-e168-floor.sh instead: `Clock` and `Timer` are
; UNINHABITED. No extern in scaffold/lib returns either one, and a porttype has
; no constructor, so no term anywhere can produce a `Clock` -- `time-mono` and
; `sleep-ms` are crossings no program can reach. Their types still refuse a
; dropped or duplicated cap (the generated family in Phase 12 covers all ten
; porttypes, these two included), which is a refusal quantified over an empty
; set of callers. Recording that as "meaning" without the reachability column
; would be the lane's own version of a gate that cannot fail.
;
; Nothing here crosses at runtime: `compile-main` calls none of them, because
; the measurement is acceptance at LOAD. exit 0 = all seven typecheck.

(import "ports/sock")
(import "ports/fd")
(import "ports/pool")
(import "runtime/proc")

; ---- Z1: the crossing that does not cross ----------------------------------
(def pe-send-noop (=> (1 s Sock) Bytes SendR)
  (lam (s bs) (send-r s)))

; ---- Z2: a capability from an integer --------------------------------------
(def pe-forge-fd (=> I64 Fd)
  (lam (i) (adopt-fd i)))

; ---- Z3: the real write, with no capability held ---------------------------
(def pe-ambient-w (=> Bytes I64)
  (lam (bs) (write-fd 1 bs)))

; ---- Z4: a PURE arrow performing a crossing --------------------------------
(def pe-pure-write (-> Bytes I64)
  (lam (bs) (write-fd 1 bs)))

; ---- Z5: an offset outside the bound the port type carries -----------------
(def pe-pool-oob (=> (1 p (Pool 4096)) (Pool 4096))
  (lam (p) (pool-write 4096 p 999999 (str->bytes "x"))))

; ---- Z6: one linear obligation, duplicated ---------------------------------
(data PeTwoReap () (pe-two-reap (1 a Reap) (1 b Reap)))

(declare pe-reap-dup (=> (w r Reap) PeTwoReap))
(def pe-reap-dup (lam (r) (pe-two-reap r r)))

; ---- Z7: a send on a socket the peer already closed ------------------------
(def pe-send-after (=> (1 s Sock) Bytes SendR)
  (lam (s bs)
    (case (sock-recv s 1)
      ((recv-r b s2)    (sock-send s2 bs))
      ((recv-closed s2) (sock-send s2 bs))
      ((recv-err m)     (send-err m)))))

(def compile-main (-> I64 I64) (lam (n) 0))
